[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This room is completely dark. I can\'t see anything. But this can see every wall, every object, and every person in this room 4,500 times every second. And it\'s not just seeing the room. Watch'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this arrow. It\'s not showing where I am right now. It\'s predicting where I will be in the next second. But before any of this worked, I had one really frustrating problem. I would plug it in,'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it would spin for about 2 seconds and then just stop every single time. The strange part, almost nobody talks about why it happens or how to fix it. Later in this video, I will show you exactly'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what was causing it and how to get around it. By the end of this video, we are going to take this tiny LAR far beyond just measuring distance. We are going to turn this affordable LAR into a system that can track moving people and'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'objects. Calculate position, distance, and movement speed. Predict where a person is going. Create a safety corridor around a moving object. Find the safest opening through obstacles.'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Predict potential collisions and calculate time to collision. Generate steering commands for a future robot. Control real hardware from LAR data. and then bring everything together inside a fully interactive 3D world running on'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the RDKX5. And every demo builds on the one before it. So by the end you will see how it all comes together into a complete robot navigation system. If you want to build it yourself, I will also'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give you all 10 Python scripts. This is the LD19. It\'s a spinning LAR sensor. So why did I choose the LD19? Well, there are cheaper LAR sensors and there are much more expensive ones, but'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I think this one is right in the middle. It\'s affordable enough to experiment with, but accurate enough to build real projects. I\'m not going to read the entire data sheet to you. You can always'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do that yourself. Instead, let\'s look at the few specifications that actually matter when you are building a project with the LD19. The first one is power. The LD19 runs on 5 volts, which sounds'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perfect because almost every development board has a 5-fold output. I first powered it from my laptop and it worked perfectly. But when I connected it to the RDKX, something strange happened.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The LAR started spinning and then stopped. If you\'re using an ESP 32, an Arduino, or another controller board, there is a good chance you will run into the same problem. Why does it happen? We'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will leave that mystery for now. One more thing worth remembering. The LD1 normally uses about 180 milliamps, but when it first starts spinning, it can briefly draw up to 300 milliamps. That'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'startup current is more important than most people realize. The next thing is the UART voltage. The data pins use 3.3 volt logic, not 5 volts. If you\'re using an ESP 32, you are good to go because it'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already works at 3.3 volts. But if you\'re using a five-fold board like an Arduino Uno, don\'t connect the TX pin directly to the LD19. Use a level shifter to protect the sensor. One last'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing, this is a class one laser, which is the safest laser classification. It\'s designed to be safe during normal use, so you don\'t need to worry about using it around yourself or the people around'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you. The rest of the specifications are on the screen if you would like to read them. The first pin is TX. This is the data output. So connected to the RX pin on your controller. Next is PWM. This'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pin is optional if you leave it disconnected. The LE19 controls its own motor speed and spins at 10 hertz by default. You only need this pin if you want to control the motor speed'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself. The third pin is ground and the fourth pin is 5 volts which powers the sensor. That\'s it. Four pins and you are ready to go. Before we write any code, there is one thing you should know. The LD19 uses a standard UART'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'serial connection. The bot rate is 230400. It uses eight data bits, one stop bit, no par, and no flow control. Now, here\'s the interesting part. You don\'t have to'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'send the sensor any commands. As soon as it powers on and starts spinning, it starts sending data all by itself. The LD19 keeps sending around 4500 measurements every second all by itself.'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And that\'s one of the reasons I like working with this LAR. Okay, this little diagram in the data sheet looks confusing, but I promise it\'s actually dead simple. Let me break it down for'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you. Think of the LD19 like a clock, but a clock that only has one hand, and that hand is a laser spinning around 10 times every second. See this arrow right here'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on top of the sensor? That\'s your zero. That\'s forward. Whatever direction this arrow is pointing when you mount it on your robot, that\'s angle zero. So straight ahead, 0°. Something on the'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'robot\'s right side, that\'s 90°. Something directly behind you, 180°. And over on the left, 270. So when the sensor sees this object here sitting a'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little to the front right, it doesn\'t just tell you, hey, there is something there. It gives you two numbers. The angle 50° and the distance 1.2 m. That\'s it. Angle and distance. That\'s literally'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the entire scan. 4,500 times a second. Now, why clockwise and not counterclockwise? Honestly, there\'s no genius engineering reason. It\'s just comes down to which way the motor inside'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spins. LD robot designed it to rotate clockwise. That\'s just how it works. Once you know that, it\'s not a problem. Just handle it in your code and everything lines up perfectly. The LD19'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also comes with this little USB adapter board. And honestly, it\'s one of my favorite things about this sensor. The board converts the LiDAR\'s UART signal into USB using a CP2102 USB to serial'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chip. That means you can plug it straight into your computer and it shows up as a normal serial port. LD robot also provides free desktop software for'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'testing the sensor. Just select your device and the bot rate is filled in automatically. Then click the play button and you will see a live point cloud on your screen. It\'s a really quick way to check that everything is'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working before you start writing code. If you\'re using Windows, you will probably need to install the CP2102 driver first. It\'s a one-time setup. You will find the driver, the software, and all the codes from this video on my'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Patreon page. The link is in the description. The official software is great for testing the sensor, but if you want to track objects, avoid obstacles, or build a robot, you will need your own code. So, I wrote five Python programs'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that run on any computer and I also wrote five more for the RDKX5. Let\'s start with the first one. This program connects directly to the LD19, reads the'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'raw serial data, and turns it into a live radar display. Let\'s run it. I\'m going to walk around the room and'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see how well the LG19 keeps up. Watch the screen. Every wall, every corner, and every object is being made lived as I move around the room. That\'s 4,500 laser measurements every second,'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building a map of the room in real time. Even when I walk straight toward the sensor, it keeps tracking everything without missing a beat. This is the same technology used in self-driving cars and warehouse robots to understand the world'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around them. Now, let me show you something really useful. See this export scan to CSV button. Watch this. With one click, the current scan is saved as a CSV file. Every angle and every distance'

[08:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from that moment is stored. Once the data is in a CSV file, you can analyze it, create your own visualizations or use it in your own projects. This image was generated from that same CSV file.'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Same skin, same data, just turned into a clean visualization that you can study, share, or include in a report. This next version doesn\'t just see the room. It'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually triggs what\'s moving in it. But before I run it, take a look. I added two new controls up here. A calibrate background button and a cluster limit slider. Here\'s why. This script works by'

[08:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comparing what\'s here right now against what was here a second ago. So before it can spot anything moving, it first needs to learn what the empty room looks like. And the cluster limit slider that'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controls how the LAR groups points together into one object. All right, let\'s run it. First thing, let\'s hit calibrate play background. Okay, done. The sensor locks onto me'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'immediately and the red circle follows me in real time. But it\'s doing more than just tracking my position. It\'s also calculating my speed. Watch the number as I walk. And now as I speed up'

[09:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and see this arrow, it\'s predicting where I\'m moving next. As long as I\'m walking, the arrow updates continuously. Now watch what happens when I stop. The red circle stays logged on me, but the'

[09:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'arrow disappears. That\'s because the circle just means this is something new, not part of the room. But the arrow is purely about speed. No movement, no velocity to show. So it just goes quiet'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'until I start moving again. Now I will walk out of the scene and bacon again. It finds me almost instantly and it doesn\'t stop at one person. It can trig'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multiple people or multiple moving objects at the same time as long as they are not part of the playground it learned during calibration. All right, tricking movement is cool, but what if'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the LAR could actually predict what\'s about to happen before it happens? That\'s exactly what this version does. It doesn\'t just track where you are. It predicts where you are going, draws a'

[10:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'safety corridor in front of you, and even calculates time to collision countdown if you are heading towards something. Before we run it, there are two sliders you should know about. Forecast distance and safety clearance.'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Forecast distance controls how far ahead the prediction goes. Right now, it\'s set to 2 m, so the sensor predicts my path 2 m in front of me. increase it for a longer prediction or reduce it to focus'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on what\'s closer. Safety clearance controls the width of the safety corridor. You can think of it as a personal safety bubble. A larger value makes the system more cautious while a'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smaller value only warns about objects directly in your path. Let\'s calibrate the background first just like before. Okay, the map is ready. Now, let\'s walk. See that dotted line in front of me?'

[11:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s my predicted path based on my current speed and direction. And those two side lines, that\'s the safety corridor. Now, watch this. I\'m going to walk straight toward this object. See'

[11:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the line turn from blue to red. And notice the label. It\'s counting down the time to collision. In other words, it\'s telling us how many seconds are left before I reach that object. If I keep'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moving at the same speed and see this impact marker, that\'s the exact point where the collision is predicted to happen. Now I will change direction. Just like that, the warning disappears.'

[12:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The countdown is gone and we are back to showing my speed. Think about what this means. This is the same basic idea used in obstacle avoidance robots and self-driving vehicles. Predict the path,'

[12:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detect the danger, and react before the collision happens. Now, this next one is a completely different idea. Forget tricking people. This version turns the liar into a self-driving robot\'s eyes.'

[12:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It scans the room, looks for gaps wide enough to drive through, and tells you which direction is clear. You will also notice two new controls, required opening width and look ahead distance.'

[12:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Watch the colors. Red means the gap is too narrow. Blue means it\'s wide enough to pass. And the green arrow, that\'s the best path. Out of all the possible openings, it picks the one closest to'

[13:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'straight ahead. But here\'s my favorite part. Look at the text on the screen. It doesn\'t just point in a direction. It gives you an actual command. Steer left, steer right, or simply forward. And it'

[13:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even tells you the exact angle. Think about what that means. It\'s the exact kind of output you could send straight to a motor controller. Read the angle, turn the wheels, and keep moving. All'

[13:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right, version five. It\'s using the same logic as version four. Same gate detection, same steering commands. The difference is that everything is now rendered in full 3D using OpenGL. Watch'

[13:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this. Same walk, same obstacles, same path planning. But now you can rotate the scene, tilt it and zoom in while the room is built in real time point by'

[13:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'point. This gives you a much better view of what the LAR is actually seeing. Now everything you have seen so far has been running on my laptop. That\'s perfect for testing. But if this lighter is going on'

[14:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a robot, a drone, or any other moving project, carrying a laptop around isn\'t exactly practical. So, let\'s move everything off the laptop and get the LD19 running on the RDKX5. Remember that'

[14:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power problem I mentioned earlier? Well, this is where I finally figured out what was going on. The first thing I tried was the simplest approach. I connected'

[14:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the LT19 to the RDKX5 using the USB adapter board. It powered on, started spinning, and about 2 seconds later, it stopped. I tried every USB port on the'

[14:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'board, but the result was always the same. It would start, then immediately shut down. That\'s when it finally clicked. The problem wasn\'t the ladder. It was the power supply. So, I decided'

[14:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to skip the USB adopter board altogether and power the LD19 directly from an external 5V 3 amps power supply. To make that clean and reusable, I actually cut'

[15:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the wires and soldered on male and female headers. Now I can just plug and unplug the interface board whenever I want instead of dealing with loose wires every single time. Here is exactly how I'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connected the LD19 to the RDKX5. The TX pin connects to UR1 RX which is pin 10. The PWM pin isn\'t being used for speed control. So I simply connected it to'

[15:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ground which is pin 9. The ground pin goes to pin 6. And finally, the 5-V pin doesn\'t connect to the RDKX5 at all. Instead, it goes directly to the external 5V 3 amps power supply. If you'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would like to build the same power supply, I have already made a dedicated video that walks through the whole process. It\'s running perfectly. No stopping, no dropouts. That was the'

[15:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole problem. It took a bit of troubleshooting to figure it out, but once you know the fix, the wiring only takes a couple of minutes. And this isn\'t only limited to the RDKX5. If you run into the same power issue with'

[16:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'another controller board, the same solution is worth trying there as well. Version one was designed to communicate with the liar through the USB adapter board. But now we have connected the'

[16:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sensor directly to the RDKX5\'s hardware. That means version one won\'t find the sensor anymore. So instead of starting there, we will jump straight to version two, which supports the native UART'

[16:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connection. All right, this is the same trick demo you saw on the laptop. The difference is now it\'s running entirely on the RDX5. I will walk around the room again and see if it can keep up just as'

[16:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well as the laptop did, tracking every movement in real time. Everything from this point on is designed to run on a real robot or any other standalone project. So stick around because the'

[17:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next few demos are where everything really comes together. Now version 3 adds something genuinely useful. A proper safety alert system. Let\'s calibrate first. Okay, the map is locked. Now I will walk'

[17:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around. Normally everything stays green because I\'m at safe distance from the walls. But now let\'s walk right up to a wall. There it is. The status turns red. And you can even see the distance to the'

[17:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wall right there on the screen. Mounted in a warehouse, a halfway near machinery, or anywhere you need an instant warning when someone or something gets too close to a boundary.'

[17:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'No cameras, no video to review, just an instant real-time warning. Now, version 4 takes things a step further. It\'s using the same path planning logic as'

[17:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before, but this time it\'s not just displaying the results on the screen. It\'s actually controlling real hardware. Take a look at this. I have connected an LED to GPIO pin 37 on the RDKX5. Watch'

[18:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the LED as I move around. When there is a clear path ahead, it stays on. But as soon as I block that path, it starts blinking. So instead LED, you can'

[18:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control a servo or motors. The possibilities are endless. All right, this is the final version and it\'s where everything comes together. It\'s using the same path planning, the same'

[18:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'collision alerts, and the same real hardware control. The difference is that everything is now rendered in a true 3D scene with walls and obstacles built up as solid shapes instead of flat points.'

[18:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Since this version uses OpenGL for 3D rendering, you will need to install a few extra Python packages first. Just run this command. That\'s it. Once it\'s installed, you are ready to go. Look at'

[18:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. It\'s incredibly smooth on the RDKX5. Honestly, it feels just like running it on my laptop, which is pretty impressive for a board that fits in the palm of your hand. At this point, I'

[19:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t even need to explain what\'s happening. You already know. If you would like to build this yourself, you will find every Python script, driver, and file I used in this video on my Patreon. So that\'s all for now. Support'

[19:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'me on Patreon for more videos. I hope you like today\'s episode. Like and share this video with your friends. See you in next episode and thanks for watching.'
